   "source": [
    "## Variable Justification from Related Literature\n",
    "\n",
    "Each explanatory variable included in this analysis is supported by peer-reviewed or institutional research.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**X1 — Healthcare Spending**\n",
    "\n",
    "Jaba, E., Balan, C. B., & Robu, I. B. (2014). The relationship between life expectancy at birth and health expenditures estimated by a cross-country and time-series analysis. *Procedia Economics and Finance*, 15, 108–114.\n",
    "\n",
    "Higher healthcare expenditure is consistently associated with improved life expectancy. Countries that invest more in health systems tend to have better medical infrastructure, preventive care, and access to treatment.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**X2 — Access to Clean Water**\n",
    "\n",
    "United Nations. (2023). *The Sustainable Development Goals Report 2023*. United Nations Publications.\n",
    "\n",
    "Access to safe drinking water is a fundamental determinant of population health. Lack of clean water is linked to preventable diseases such as cholera and diarrhea, which directly reduce life expectancy, especially in low-income countries.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**X3 — GDP per Capita**\n",
    "\n",
    "Preston, S. H. (1975). The changing relation between mortality and level of economic development. *Population Studies*, 29(2), 231–248.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Preston Curve is one of the most well-established relationships in demography — wealthier nations consistently exhibit longer life expectancy due to greater access to food, housing, sanitation, and healthcare.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**X4 — CO2 Emissions**\n",
    "\n",
    "Wen, J., Mughal, N., Zhao, J., & Hayat, M. (2021). Does globalization matter for environmental degradation? Nexus among energy consumption, economic growth, and carbon dioxide emission. *Energy Policy*, 153, 112230.\n",
    "\n",
    "Higher CO2 emissions reflect environmental degradation and industrial pollution, which contribute to respiratory diseases, cardiovascular conditions, and reduced life quality. Several studies have found a negative association between CO2 emissions and population health outcomes."
   ]
  },
