   "source": [
    "## Interpretation of Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Drivers of Life Expectancy (RLM, standardized coefficients)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Rank | Variable | Std. Beta | p-value | Significant (p < 0.05) | Direction vs. literature |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | CO2 Emissions | -2.08 | < 0.001 | Yes | Matches (negative) |\n",
    "| 2 | Water Access | -1.04 | < 0.001 | Yes | **Opposite** of expected (negative) |\n",
    "| 3 | Healthcare Spending | +0.79 | 0.036 | Yes | Matches (positive) |\n",
    "| 4 | GDP per Capita | -0.35 | 0.215 | No | Not significant |\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "Standardized betas beyond +/-1 are a symptom of the multicollinearity noted above (overlapping predictors pull each other's coefficients apart), so rank the variables by sign and significance rather than treating the sizes as exact.\n",
    "\n",
    "**CO2 Emissions** has the largest effect: one standard deviation more CO2 per capita is associated with about 2 standard deviations lower life expectancy, consistent with the pollution-health link (Wen et al., 2021). CO2 also separates the two countries (UK averages 7.3 t, US 17.0 t), so part of this effect reflects the UK/US gap.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Water Access** is significant but **negative**, which contradicts the expected direction (United Nations, 2023). The cause is the data, not the theory: water access only ranges from 98.9% to 100% (the UK is 100% in every year), so the variable behaves as a country marker and is strongly correlated with CO2 (r = -0.87). It should not be read as clean water harming health.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Healthcare Spending** has a positive, significant effect under robust regression (p = 0.036), in line with Jaba et al. (2014). Under plain OLS it is not significant (p = 0.386), so this finding is sensitive to the model choice.\n",
    "\n",
    "**GDP per Capita** is not significant. It is highly correlated with Healthcare Spending (r = 0.90), so the two share the same information (VIF 16.8 and 29.4).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "Controlling for the other variables, **CO2 emissions** and **healthcare spending** behave as the literature predicts, while **water access** shows a counter-intuitive sign caused by almost no variation in the data, and **GDP per capita** adds no independent explanatory power. The model explains about 78-80% of the variation in life expectancy (OLS R2 = 0.80, RLM R2 = 0.78).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** only two countries (UK, US) and 44 observations; yearly data from the same country are not independent; high multicollinearity; results show association, not causation. Adding more countries would make the water access and country effects estimable.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Dashboard Link:** https://sdg3-life-expectancy-dashboard-78h7lhgxxm3uormh8skbtu.streamlit.app/
   ]
  },
